{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"Long SEZABY boxed-warning text in row; source currently points to FDA/DailyMed SPL and DailyMed URL.","fda_source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%228c7d0402-4977-4c25-bc4b-11db91339e9a%22&limit=5"],"proposed_value":"Keep boxed-warning content, but replace the source metadata with FDA/openFDA or Drugs@FDA/accessdata FDA labeling. Do not cite DailyMed for this field.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports the SEZABY boxed warning for concomitant opioids, dependence/withdrawal after longer-than-recommended use, and abuse/misuse/addiction with unapproved adolescent/adult use. The content is supported, but the source field violates the audit rule because it cites DailyMed.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"phenobarbital","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"phenobarbital","rationale":"Correct active moiety; FDA SEZABY documents identify phenobarbital sodium and NCBI sources identify phenobarbital/phenobarbitone as the same ASM.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2023/215910Orig1s000Approv.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548269/"]},{"current_value":"phenobarbitone","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"phenobarbitone","rationale":"Phenobarbitone is a standard alternate generic spelling used in PubMed/NCBI literature for the same drug.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2597/?report=reader"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"phenobarbitone","rationale":"Important PubMed records, including Bacon1981, use phenobarbitone. Leaving this blank risks missing same-drug records and should not create a duplicate drug row.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/"]},{"current_value":"Luminal; Phenobarb; Solfoton","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Luminal; Phenobarb; Solfoton; Sezaby","rationale":"FDA review supports historical Luminal and FDA approval/orphan records support current SEZABY. Phenobarb/Solfoton were not confirmed from FDA sources in this audit, but no direct contradiction was found, so do not delete them without user approval.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2023/215910Orig1s000MedR.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=703819","https://www.epilepsy.com/stories/summary-anti-seizure-medications"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us / filter_availability","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"SEZABY was FDA approved for neonatal seizures on 2022-11-17, and FDA review notes marketed U.S. phenobarbital formulations.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2023/215910Orig1s000Approv.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=703819"]},{"current_value":"1912 legacy; predates modern FDA approval system","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"1912 legacy marketing under Luminal before modern FDA approval; SEZABY NDA approved 11/17/2022 for neonatal seizures","rationale":"FDA clinical review states phenobarbital was synthesized in 1911 and marketed in 1912 as Luminal, before modern FDA approval requirements, and FDA approval documents give SEZABY approval date 2022-11-17.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2023/215910Orig1s000MedR.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2023/215910Orig1s000Approv.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Tablet; elixir/oral solution; IV/IM injection","field":"formulations_available / filter_formulation","proposed_value":"Tablet; elixir/oral solution; IV/IM injection","rationale":"NCBI StatPearls lists oral elixir, oral tablets, and IM/IV solution; FDA SEZABY label supports IV injection formulation.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532277/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"53-118 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"Adults about 75-120 h; children about 21-75 h; neonates approximately 1 week","rationale":"The selected FDA label does not support 53-118 h; FDA labeling states neonatal t1/2 is about 1 week, and eMC SmPC reports adult half-life about 75-120 h and pediatric about 21-75 h. Current value should be replaced or separately sourced if retained.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/3607/smpc"]},{"current_value":"Liver","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism / filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver/hepatic","rationale":"FDA label states phenobarbital is metabolized by CYP2C9 and lesser CYP2C19/CYP2E1/UGTs; NCBI states hepatic microsomal metabolism.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532277/"]},{"current_value":"Strong enzyme inducer: CYP and UGT pathways","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting / filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"Strong enzyme inducer: CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2C family and UGT pathways","rationale":"FDA label identifies induction of CYP3A4, CYP2B6, CYP2Cs and UGTs; eMC notes induction of hepatic drug-metabolizing enzymes.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/4920/smpc"]},{"current_value":"Mechanism is not fully understood; barbiturate potentiation of synaptic inhibition through GABA-A receptors is the principal accepted antiseizure mechanism.","field":"mechanism_of_action / filter_mechanism / mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Mechanism is not fully understood; barbiturate potentiation of synaptic inhibition through GABA-A receptors is the principal accepted antiseizure mechanism.","rationale":"FDA label states the precise mechanism is not fully understood and is thought to involve potentiation of synaptic inhibition through GABAA receptors; NCBI describes increased duration of GABA-A chloride-channel opening.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532277/"]},{"current_value":"Focal; Generalized tonic-clonic; Neonatal seizures; Status epilepticus","field":"epilepsy_type / filter_epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal; Generalized tonic-clonic; Neonatal seizures; Status epilepticus","rationale":"FDA supports neonatal seizures; NCBI sources support use for partial/focal and generalized seizures and status epilepticus/established convulsive status epilepticus. Absence seizures should remain excluded.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548269/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2597/?report=reader","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539731/"]},{"current_value":"Adults often 60-200 mg/day once daily or divided; pediatric weight-based","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Adults often 60-200 mg/day once daily or divided; pediatric weight-based","rationale":"eMC supports usual adult daily dose 60-200 mg in divided doses and pediatric 3-6 mg/kg/day; NCBI epilepsy text supports adult 50-200 mg nocte and children 3-5 mg/kg/day.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/4920/smpc","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2597/?report=reader"]},{"current_value":"Individualized by serum level/tolerability; commonly 60 mg/day adult initial range","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Individualized by serum level/tolerability; commonly 60 mg/day adult initial range","rationale":"NCBI LiverTox states typical adult starting dose for seizures is 60-100 mg daily in divided doses; eMC supports 60-200 mg/day usual adult dosing.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548269/","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/4920/smpc"]},{"current_value":"Individualized by serum level/tolerability; no single fixed max","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"Depends on indication/formulation: SEZABY neonatal regimen maximum total loading dose 40 mg/kg and maintenance 4.5 mg/kg/day for up to 5 days; oral epilepsy SmPC usual adult daily dose 60-200 mg and pediatric 3-6 mg/kg/day","rationale":"The current text is too vague for approved/labelled dosing. FDA SEZABY label provides a maximum loading dose and maintenance regimen; eMC provides usual oral adult and pediatric dosing.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/4920/smpc"]},{"current_value":"No direct QT effect established","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"QTc effect not adequately characterized; FDA label warns to avoid concomitant use in patients at significant torsade de pointes risk and with drugs/products that may increase QTc-prolongation risk","rationale":"FDA label has a QT Prolongation warning and states SEZABY's QTc effect has not been adequately characterized, with nonclinical hERG signal and QTc prolongation reported in adult literature. Current wording is misleading.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS: drowsiness/sedation 20-60%, behavioral: hyperactivity/irritability 5-15%, dermatologic: rash 1-3%, respiratory: respiratory depression <1% at therapeutic doses","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: sedation 16% in neonatal Study 1; respiratory: abnormal respiration 25% in neonatal Study 1 and respiratory depression reported/warned; GI/feeding: feeding disorder 16%; cardiovascular: hypotension 16%; behavioral/psychiatric symptoms including abnormal behavior, aggression, hyperactivity, agitation and irritability reported, frequency not reliably estimable; dermatologic reactions including rash, SJS/TEN and DRESS reported, frequency not reliably estimable","rationale":"FDA label provides specific neonatal trial percentages that do not match the row. eMC supports behavioral and dermatologic categories but not the row's percentages. The respiratory '<1%' statement is not supported and conflicts with FDA neonatal abnormal-respiration incidence.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/4920/smpc"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling; American Epilepsy Society 2024 U.S. ASM summary; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA/Drugs@FDA SEZABY label and approval review; FDA/openFDA SEZABY label API; NCBI Bookshelf LiverTox phenobarbital; NCBI Bookshelf StatPearls phenobarbital/barbiturates; eMC/medicines.org.uk Phenobarbital Elixir BP and Phenobarbital Sodium Injection SmPCs; Epilepsy Foundation ASM summary; PubMed RCT records for Takami2019, Crawley2000 and Bacon1981","rationale":"Current evidence_sources includes DailyMed for FDA labeling and non-whitelisted sources. Replace with trusted domains actually used to support row facts.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%228c7d0402-4977-4c25-bc4b-11db91339e9a%22&limit=5","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548269/","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/4920/smpc","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/3607/smpc","https://www.epilepsy.com/stories/summary-anti-seizure-medications"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling; American Epilepsy Society 2024 U.S. ASM summary; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/Drugs@FDA SEZABY label; NCBI Bookshelf Phenobarbital StatPearls","rationale":"Mechanism is supportable from FDA labeling and NCBI. DailyMed should be removed from the source wording; AES/Sills-Rogawski were not independently verified in this audit from whitelisted source URLs.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532277/"]}],"generic_name":"phenobarbital","missing_rcts":[{"pmid":"","proposed_link_text":"Do not add to pubmed_phase_ii_iii_rct_links unless a PMID/publication is identified; consider a separate ClinicalTrials.gov evidence field if unpublished trials are tracked.","title":"Evaluation Phenobarbital as Adjunctive Therapy in Participants With Partial Onset Seizures (NCT01284556)","url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT01284556","why_relevant":"ClinicalTrials.gov describes a completed phase 3 randomized quadruple-masked placebo-controlled phenobarbital adjunctive trial, but no PMID was identified in the provided bundle."}],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"83.3 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Takami2019 phenobarbital 10 mg/kg IV 83.3%)","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"83.3 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom differential: Takami2019 phenobarbital 10 mg/kg IV, 7/7 active seizure-free vs 1/6 placebo seizure-free)","rationale":"Takami2019 abstract reports no post-dose seizures in 7 phenobarbital patients and seizures after placebo in 5/6 placebo patients, so seizure freedom is 100.0% vs 16.7%, differential 83.3 percentage points.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30954360/"]},{"current_value":"Takami2019|83.3|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30954360/|13","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Takami2019|83.3|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30954360/|13","rationale":"The plotted value matches the extractable Takami2019 seizure-freedom differential and total randomized n=13.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30954360/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"Takami2019 reports post-dose seizure recurrence/seizure-freedom, Crawley2000 reports three-or-more seizures and mortality, and Bacon1981 reports febrile-convulsion recurrence; no RR50 differential is extractable. Remove 'phase II/III' wording unless strict phase evidence is added.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30954360/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10703801/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo median percent-change differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"None of the three retained PubMed RCTs provides an extractable median percent seizure-frequency change differential. Remove 'phase II/III' wording unless strict phase evidence is added.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30954360/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10703801/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/"]}],"overall_status":"needs_update","proposed_row_updates":[{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"phenobarbitone","reason":"Important same-drug PubMed literature uses the non-US spelling.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/"]},{"current_value":"Luminal; Phenobarb; Solfoton","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Luminal; Phenobarb; Solfoton; Sezaby","reason":"SEZABY is an FDA-approved phenobarbital sodium trade name; no deletion proposed for unverified names without direct contradiction.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=703819"]},{"current_value":"No direct QT effect established","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"QTc effect not adequately characterized; FDA label warns to avoid concomitant use in patients at significant torsade de pointes risk and with drugs/products that may increase QTc-prolongation risk","reason":"FDA label contains QT Prolongation warning and contradicts the current reassuring wording.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"53-118 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"Adults about 75-120 h; children about 21-75 h; neonates approximately 1 week","reason":"Current numeric range was not supported by the selected FDA/openFDA source; trusted FDA/eMC sources support different population-specific values.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/3607/smpc"]},{"current_value":"Individualized by serum level/tolerability; no single fixed max","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"Depends on indication/formulation: SEZABY neonatal regimen maximum total loading dose 40 mg/kg and maintenance 4.5 mg/kg/day for up to 5 days; oral epilepsy SmPC usual adult daily dose 60-200 mg and pediatric 3-6 mg/kg/day","reason":"FDA/eMC labeling provides labelled regimen limits and usual dose ranges.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/4920/smpc"]}],"rct_link_checks":[{"label":"Takami2019","pmid":"30954360","proposed_action":"Retain as a verified phenobarbital placebo-controlled RCT; add caveat that PubMed does not explicitly label it phase II/III.","rationale":"PMID, title, drug, randomized single-blind placebo-controlled design, 10 mg/kg IV phenobarbital vs normal saline placebo, and n=13 match the row/outcome extraction.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30954360/"},{"label":"Crawley2000","pmid":"10703801","proposed_action":"Retain as a verified phenobarbital placebo-controlled RCT, but do not use for RR50/MPC/seizure-freedom extraction.","rationale":"The trial is phenobarbital 20 mg/kg IM vs identical placebo in childhood cerebral malaria; endpoint is seizure prophylaxis using three-or-more seizures and mortality, not RR50/MPC/seizure freedom. PubMed does not explicitly label phase II/III.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10703801/"},{"label":"Bacon1981","pmid":"6116084","proposed_action":"Retain as a verified phenobarbitone/phenobarbital placebo-controlled RCT, but do not use for RR50/MPC/seizure-freedom extraction from abstract-only data.","rationale":"The publication is a placebo-controlled phenobarbitone and phenytoin prophylaxis trial in febrile convulsions with phenobarbitone, phenytoin, and placebo arms. PubMed does not explicitly label phase II/III.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/"}],"source_updates":[{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=boxed_warning_found; setid=8c7d0402-4977-4c25-bc4b-11db91339e9a; published=; title=These highlights do not include all the information needed to use SEZABY safely and effectively. See full prescribing information for SEZABY. SEZABY™ (phenobarbital sodium) for injection, for intravenous use, CIV Initial U.S. Approval: 2022; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=8c7d0402-4977-4c25-bc4b-11db91339e9a","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA drug label API; status=boxed_warning_found; spl_set_id=8c7d0402-4977-4c25-bc4b-11db91339e9a; effective_time=20251223; title=SEZABY / PHENOBARBITAL SODIUM; api_url=https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%228c7d0402-4977-4c25-bc4b-11db91339e9a%22&limit=5","reason":"DailyMed is not permissible for FDA boxed-warning verification; use FDA/openFDA or accessdata FDA labeling.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%228c7d0402-4977-4c25-bc4b-11db91339e9a%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling; American Epilepsy Society 2024 U.S. ASM summary; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/Drugs@FDA SEZABY label; NCBI Bookshelf Phenobarbital StatPearls","reason":"The mechanism fact is supported by FDA and NCBI sources; DailyMed and unverified non-whitelisted sources should not be the named support.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/215910s000lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532277/"]}],"summary":"The row is for the correct drug and the three PubMed links are same-drug placebo-controlled RCTs. Updates are needed for FDA boxed-warning source metadata, missing Sezaby/phenobarbitone aliases, QT warning wording, adverse-event percentages, half-life, maximum-dose wording, and source lists that currently cite DailyMed or non-whitelisted sources."}
